{
  "heading": "Use it in another page",
  "body": "A page that is not a Tac page loads the element with one `<script type=\"module\">` tag and then uses the tag. Serve the page from the same origin as the build output. The element starts its services as soon as it joins the page, like `hydrate=\"load\"`. Several elements on one page share one Tac runtime.\n\n- Props in: an attribute gives a string. Its name is the prop name in kebab case, so `persistId` is the attribute `persist-id`. A JavaScript property with the prop name takes any value: a number, an object or an array. React, Angular and Vue set properties on custom elements, so they pass rich values. The last write wins. When a prop changes, the view shows the new value. A field that a service needs is read once, when the service starts.\n- Events out: listen with `addEventListener('count-changed', ...)`. `event.detail` is a copy of the model value. The event bubbles and leaves the element, so a listener on `document` hears it too. When each element has its own service (`@NeedsAll` or `@Lifetime('prototype')`), each element sends its own events.\n- Slot content: the children of the tag stay in your page, and the browser shows them where the template has `<slot>`. A child with a `slot` attribute, such as `<p slot=\"note\">`, shows in the named slot `<slot name=\"note\">` instead. Your framework keeps control of them.\n- Styles: the element draws its view in a shadow root (a separate DOM tree inside the element), with the component styles. The styles of your page do not reach inside, except inherited values such as `color` and `font`, and CSS custom properties such as `--shop-accent`. Use custom properties to theme an element.\n\nServices in compiled languages (WebAssembly), serving the element from another origin and a `hydrate` attribute are planned.",
  "code": {
    "file": "host.html"
  }
}
